\title{gllvmTMB: Fit Multivariate Models from Wide Response Data}
\description{
\if{html}{\figure{logo.png}{options: style='float: right' alt='logo' width='120'}}

Fits multivariate models for data where each site, individual, species, or study has several responses. Start from a wide data frame with the 'traits()' formula interface, or from already-stacked long data, and estimate trait covariance, pairwise correlations, shared latent axes, and trait-specific variance. Binary presence-absence applications include binomial-family joint species distribution models that decompose environmental and residual co-occurrence in multispecies SDMs (Pollock et al. 2014 \doi{10.1111/2041-210X.12180}). Optional animal, phylogenetic, spatial, and known-sampling-variance components use the same formula grammar. Model fitting uses Template Model Builder (Kristensen et al. 2016 \doi{10.18637/jss.v070.i05}); spatial helpers implement the stochastic partial differential equation/Gaussian Markov random field (SPDE/GMRF) construction of Lindgren, Rue, and Lindstrom (2011), and phylogenetic relatedness follows Hadfield & Nakagawa (2010) \doi{10.1111/j.1420-9101.2009.01915.x}. This package is experimental: it is not complete, is not fully human-verified, and needs extensive further validation, so it should be used at your own risk. Point estimates are the primary output, and how well they are supported depends on the exact model and route used. Broad package-wide interval coverage is not yet confirmed. A handful of interval calculations for standardized factor loadings have been checked and shown accurate, but only for one Gaussian model, in a few fixed sample-size and rank combinations, fitted to one fixed simulated dataset with known true values, and only for fits that converge cleanly; this does not carry over to any other sample size, rank, or dataset. Total-variance penalty profiles are still only an approximate calculation: even in previously checked cases, we have not confirmed they match the exact answer.
}
